      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Foundation Repair Methods', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/foundation-repair-methods' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Monitoring', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/monitoring-deep-dive' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="minimal"
  schema={schema}
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Monitoring"
  nextPage={{ path: '/foundation-repair-methods', name: 'Foundation Repair Methods' }}
>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Monitoring</span>
        <h1>Monitoring —<br /><em>When Waiting Is the Right Call</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Not every issue needs immediate intervention. What monitoring involves, and why it's a real recommendation, not a stall tactic.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">Monitoring is the one item in the toolkit that isn't a repair at all — it's a decision to wait and watch, made deliberately rather than by default. That distinction matters more than it sounds like it should.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-it-is">
          <h2>Measurements Over Time, <em>Not a One-Time Check</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Monitoring means tracking a foundation's elevation or crack width over time at fixed points — not a one-time visual check, but repeated measurements spaced out over weeks or months.</p>
          <p>A floor-level survey is one of the most useful tools for this, but it's worth being precise about what a single survey actually proves: one survey is one data point, a snapshot of the floor's elevation at that moment. It takes two, sometimes three, surveys spaced out over time to see whether anything is actually changing and by how much — a single reading can't answer that on its own, no matter how detailed it is.</p>
          <p>Crack width works the same way, tracked with a crack monitor at a fixed location and compared over time to establish a pattern: is the movement active and progressing, has it stabilized, or is it seasonal — expanding and contracting with moisture cycles rather than trending in one direction. TLS sells and installs crack monitors directly, and for anyone who wants to start tracking a crack on their own before deciding on anything else, our free <a href="/crack-gauge" class="body-link">printable Crack Gauge</a> is a straightforward way to begin. [TIM: roughly how long does a monitoring period actually run before you have enough data to call it one way or the other — weeks, a full seasonal cycle, something else?]</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="why-it-is-legitimate">
          <h2>Why This Is a <em>Legitimate Recommendation</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">A company that only makes money on repairs has no incentive to ever tell a homeowner to wait — which is exactly why it's worth naming plainly when waiting genuinely is the right answer.</p>
          <p>Not every crack needs repair. Seasonal movement can stabilize on its own. Some movement is cosmetic rather than structural. Monitoring is how you find out which one you're actually looking at, instead of guessing — or being sold a repair for a problem that was never going to get worse.</p>
          <p>In practice, a few concrete signs point toward monitoring being the right first move: symptoms that look old and haven't changed, a pattern that's seasonal and partly recovers, a moisture source that was just corrected, or a house that still functions with no growing offsets at doors or load-bearing points. Monitoring stops being enough once change is documented and one-way, a load-bearing element is actively opening or offsetting, or doors, floors, and exterior lines are moving together — those are reasons to act, not keep watching.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="timing">
          <h2>Start Early, <em>Especially Before a Sale</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Monitoring only works if there's time for it to work — which is exactly why the sooner it starts after a problem is noticed, the more useful it is.</p>
          <p>A home that's already on the market or under contract usually doesn't have room left to watch a crack for a few months and see what happens. A buyer, their lender, or their inspector wants an answer now, not a pattern that takes a season to develop — so if monitoring hasn't already started by the time a sale is underway, there typically isn't time left to start it and still have something to show.</p>
          <p>That's the case for starting sooner rather than later, not a reason to put it off. The earlier monitoring begins, the sooner there's real data behind whatever gets said about the movement — either evidence that it has continued, which points toward a repair, or evidence that it's stopped, which can support leaving it alone. Either one is a stronger position for a seller than "we don't know yet," which is where things end up if monitoring never started at all. Piers aren't always needed — but starting the monitoring process early, in a logical and documented way, is almost always a good option.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="after-a-repair">
          <h2>Monitoring Doesn't Stop <em>After a Repair</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Everything above is about the period before a repair decision. Monitoring changes purpose once work is finished — from deciding whether to act, to confirming the repair is doing what it was designed to do.</p>
          <p>A repair addresses the condition it was designed for; it doesn't guarantee no other condition will ever affect the house again, and some settling of finishes, minor crack changes, or door adjustment can be normal in the months afterward as the house settles into its new support. What's reasonable to watch afterward: elevation readings at the same points measured before the repair, whether existing cracks stay stable and whether new cracking appears outside the repaired area, whether adjusted doors and windows keep operating normally, and drainage and grading around the house, since water management is often part of what keeps a repair working over time. A single hairline crack or one sticking door a year later isn't automatically evidence of failure — a pattern of renewed movement at the same points that were repaired is what's worth raising with whoever did the work.</p>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a> · <a href="/glossary#monitoring" class="body-link">Glossary: Monitoring →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

